Z||000000||FAXKID||06-02-99||07:42 PM||||COULD YOU TELL ME WHAT A FAIR PRICE FOR A REBUILD WOULD COST FOR A V6 1986 GT .EVEN A BALL PARK FIGURE WOULD HELP.
Z||000001||Punkndrublic||06-02-99||09:11 PM||||I had mine professionally rebuilt by my dads friend. He owns a local machine shop, and rebuilts the engines of some of the fastest cars at local track. Anyways, all i did was give him the car and he did all the rest from removing  the engine to putting it all back together again, he also glass beaded my intake and valve covers for free (at least 400 dollars worth of work in labour) he took his sweet time doing the whole thing, at least i know it was machined to close tolerances. He also replaced the clutch, two sensors and the oil pan, painted everthing cept the intake and valve covers ( which i did). Amazingly the crank was near perfect and all it needed was polishing, and the rods were fine so they were balanced and wieghted everything else was replaced. All the hardware i got at cost and the labour was vastly reduced. The whole deal cost me $ 2750 cdn no tax of course, and he said he actually lost over a 1k doing the job and would never ever do one again. So basically double that price for what i got if you unfortunatly don't know ppl and end up paying retail for everthing.
Z||000002||FieroMike||06-03-99||11:33 AM||||I took my engine out and the price for rebuilding is $1,077.00, this includes re-boring it to 3.1 liters and a performance cam.  They really charge a lot if they take out the engine... 2,200.00 I took mine out in about 6 hours total, so if you can do it, I would suggest saving 1.2K!
Z||000003||Phil||06-03-99||01:15 PM||||FieroMike The stroke of the crank makes it into a 3.1. The bore size is the same, the wrist pins are in a different place. If you want a 3.2 you need a 3.1 crank and .030 over 3.1 pistons
Z||000004||FieroMike||06-03-99||04:26 PM||||Phil,<br>In most cases that is true, they are putting a stroker kit on it, but they are over-boring it as well.  It's going to be a little closer to 3.2 liters. Sorry about that. I also heard that you can use a piston that is shorter, and leave the piston rod the same length.  No thanks. :-)<br>
